</Fragment>

<ServiceHero
  section="services"
  slug="refrigerator-repair/frigidaire-not-cooling"
  eyebrow="Frigidaire Refrigerator Not Cooling · Diagnostic Guide · Same Day Southern California"
  title="Frigidaire Refrigerator Not Cooling? Start With the Coils"
  subtitle="Most Frigidaire no-cool calls are dirty coils, the evaporator fan, or the control board — a known Frigidaire weak point. Here's what to check, what needs a tech, and how long your food stays safe."
  heroImageTitle="Frigidaire Refrigerator Not Cooling Repair"
  showBranches={true}
/>

<!-- 01 ANSWER-FIRST -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">01 · The short answer</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A running-but-warm Frigidaire usually starts with the coils.</h2>

    <p><strong>When a Frigidaire refrigerator runs but won't cool, the most common cause is dirty condenser coils that can't shed the compressor's heat — often the whole fix. Next come a failed evaporator fan ("freezer cold, fridge warm") and the electronic control board, which is a documented Frigidaire and Electrolux weak point (a dead evaporator fan often traces back to a bad board). A locked compressor or sealed-system fault is the serious end. Cleaning the coils and checking airflow are safe to do yourself; the board, fan, and sealed system are a tech job. Treat a no-cool fridge as a same-day call — your food is on a clock. $89 residential diagnostic, waived with repair.</strong></p>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Food is on a clock:</strong> per USDA and FDA guidance, a refrigerator keeps food safe for about 4 hours with the door closed. Keep the doors shut, move critical items to a cooler with ice if you can, and get a tech out before that window closes. Full food-safety details are in the section below.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 02 CONTROL BOARD (differentiator) -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">02 · The Frigidaire control board</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A known weak point that mimics other faults.</h2>

    <p>On Frigidaire and Electrolux refrigerators, the main electronic control board is one of the more frequently cited no-cool failure points. The board runs the compressor cycling, the fans, and the defrost timing, so when it fails the symptoms can look like several different parts at once — a fan that won't spin, a compressor that won't cycle, or temperatures that swing.</p>

    <p>That's why our techs test the board rather than assuming it's a simple fan or relay. Swapping an evaporator fan on a fridge whose board isn't powering it just leaves you with the same warm fridge and a wasted part. We confirm whether the board is driving the cooling system correctly first, then replace the part that actually failed. It's a tech diagnosis, but getting it right the first time is the difference between one visit and two.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 03 CAUSES -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">03 · Why a Frigidaire stops cooling</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">The causes, in the order our techs check them.</h2>

    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Dirty condenser coils (the #1 cause)</h3>
      <p>Coils caked in dust and pet hair can't release the compressor's heat, so the fridge slowly loses cooling. On Frigidaire this is the most common cause and often the entire fix — worse in an LA summer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Evaporator fan — "freezer cold, fridge warm"</h3>
      <p>The evaporator fan pushes cold air from the freezer coil up into the fridge. When it fails — or when the control board stops powering it — the freezer stays cold but the fridge warms. A tech job inside the cabinet.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Electronic control board</h3>
      <p>The documented Frigidaire weak point. A failed board can stop the fans, the compressor cycling, or the defrost, and it's the reason a "dead fan" symptom sometimes isn't the fan at all. Tech diagnosis and replacement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Locked compressor / sealed system</h3>
      <p>If the compressor won't start or runs without cooling, the sealed system is the fault — a gauges-and-tech diagnosis, and the higher-ticket end of the list.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cause-block">
      <h3>Defrost system iced over</h3>
      <p>A failed defrost heater or sensor lets frost build on the evaporator until air can't pass and cooling drops. The tell is a wall of frost behind the freezer's back panel.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 04 SAFE FIX -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">04 · What you can safely check</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Try these before the food is at risk.</h2>

    <ol class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Vacuum the condenser coils</strong> behind the rear panel or under a front kick-plate. On Frigidaire this is the top cause and often the whole fix.</li>
      <li><strong>Clear the interior vents</strong> so food isn't blocking airflow between compartments.</li>
      <li><strong>Check the door seal and temperature setting,</strong> and confirm the setting wasn't bumped warm.</li>
      <li><strong>Give it room and a power-cycle</strong> — pull the fridge off the wall, unplug a minute, restart.</li>
      <li><strong>Note the symptoms</strong> — freezer cold while fridge is warm, or erratic temps — so we can tell a fan fault from a board fault faster.</li>
    </ol>

    <p class="callout"><strong>Where to stop:</strong> coils, vents, seal, temperature, and a power-cycle are safe. Do not open the sealed refrigeration system, test the compressor, or replace the control board — those carry shock and refrigerant risks and are our techs' work.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 05 FOOD SAFETY -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">05 · Heat-wave food safety — when to call today</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">A warm fridge is a clock. Here are the numbers.</h2>

    <p>A refrigerator that's stopped cooling is a race against the clock, and in an LA heat wave that clock runs faster. Per USDA and FDA guidance:</p>

    <ul class="diag-list">
      <li><strong>Refrigerator: about 4 hours</strong> of safe food with the door kept closed.</li>
      <li><strong>Freezer: roughly 48 hours full, 24 hours half-full,</strong> if the door stays shut.</li>
      <li><strong>Above 40°F for 2 hours or more → discard it.</strong> That covers meat, poultry, seafood, eggs, milk, cut produce, and leftovers.</li>
      <li><strong>When in doubt, throw it out</strong> — and never taste food to decide. Frozen food can be safely refrozen only if it still has ice crystals or is at 40°F or below.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>That's why a no-cool Frigidaire is a same-day call. Keep the doors closed, move the critical items to a cooler with ice, and get a tech out before the four-hour fridge window closes. We answer phones 24/7 and run same-day across most of the area. <strong>$89 residential diagnostic, waived with the repair.</strong></p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 06 TECH + LINKS -->
<section class="section section-gray">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">06 · When to call us</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Coils clean, still warm? Now it's a diagnostic.</h2>

    <p>When the coils are clean, the vents are clear, and the Frigidaire still won't cool, the fault is inside — the control board, the evaporator fan, the defrost system, or the compressor and sealed system. Our techs test the board and fan together (so a bad board doesn't get misread as a bad fan), and carry the common Frigidaire fan motors and boards for a one-visit fix where possible.</p>

    <p>More help: the general <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/not-cooling/">refrigerator not cooling</a> guide (all brands), and if it's the fridge warm while the freezer's fine, <a href="/services/refrigerator-repair/fridge-not-cooling-freezer-works/">fridge not cooling, freezer works</a>. Brand coverage: <a href="/brands/frigidaire-refrigerator-repair/">Frigidaire refrigerator repair</a>. Same-day across <a href="/los-angeles/refrigerator-repair/">Los Angeles</a>, <a href="/beverly-hills/refrigerator-repair/">Beverly Hills</a>, and <a href="/rancho-cucamonga/refrigerator-repair/">Rancho Cucamonga</a> in most cases. <a href="/book/">Book online</a> or call {phone}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 07 FAQ -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="section-number">07 · FAQ</p>
    <h2 class="section-title">Frigidaire refrigerator not cooling: common questions.</h2>

    <div class="faq-list">
      {faqs.map(f => (
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>{f.q}</summary>
          <p>{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 08 BOTTOM CTA -->
